{
  "id": "positional-encoding",
  "title": "Implement Positional Encoding (sin/cos)",
  "description": "Implement sinusoidal positional encodings as described in \"Attention Is All You Need\" to inject sequence order into token embeddings.\n\nGiven a sequence length and model dimension, compute the positional encoding matrix using the sin/cos formulation.\n\n### Mathematical Definition\n\nFor position pos and dimension index i:\n\n$$\nPE(pos, 2i) = \\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{pos}{base^{\\,2i/d_{model}}}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nPE(pos, 2i+1) = \\cos\\!\\left(\\frac{pos}{base^{\\,2i/d_{model}}}\\right)\n$$\n\nEven-indexed columns use sine, odd-indexed columns use cosine, and the frequency decreases with dimension index.",
  "seoDescription": "Generate sinusoidal Transformer positional encodings across sequence positions and embedding dimensions.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: seq_len = 3, d_model = 4\n\nOutput: [[0.0000, 1.0000, 0.0000, 1.0000], [0.8415, 0.5403, 0.0100, 0.9999], [0.9093, -0.4161, 0.0200, 0.9998]]\n\nExplanation: Columns alternate sine and cosine values. For the second frequency, the divisor is 100, producing the smaller angles 0.01 and 0.02.",
    "Input: seq_len = 5, d_model = 7, base = 10000.0\n\nOutput: NumPy array of shape (5, 7)"
  ],
  "requirements": "* <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Fully vectorized (no Python loops over positions or dimensions)</span>\n* <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Support any seq_len ≥ 1 and d_model ≥ 1</span>\n* <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">For odd d_model, the last column should be the sin column</span>\n* <span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Return dtype=float for stable downstream use</span>",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Implement Positional Encoding (sin/cos)\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of artificial intelligence. Image pixels, word embeddings, and hidden representations are all structured as multi-dimensional vectors and matrices. **Implement Positional Encoding (sin/cos)** allows machine learning practitioners to project representations into informative subspaces, rotate coordinate frames, or decompose complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nIn this problem, the objective is to implement **Implement Positional Encoding (sin/cos)** efficiently and accurately adhering to standard mathematical conventions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe operation is governed by the following mathematical formulation:\n\n$$\nPE(pos, 2i) = \\sin\\!\\left(\\frac{pos}{base^{\\,2i/d_{model}}}\\right)\n$$\n\nEvery variable and index in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1: Input Validation & Pre-processing**: Validate tensor dimensions, convert inputs into contiguous array buffers, and extract structural shape parameters.\n2. **Phase 2: Core Transformation**: Apply vectorized operations directly across array dimensions without explicit Python loops.\n3. **Phase 3: Aggregation & Normalization**: Compute required summations, projections, or element-wise bounds while safeguarding against numerical instability.\n4. **Phase 4: Output Formatting**: Ensure the resulting array or dictionary matches expected output data types and precision tolerances.\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Dimensionality & Subspaces**: Orthogonal projections and low-rank factorizations compress massive feature vectors while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Condition Numbers & Inversion**: Inverting ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with near-zero determinants) causes numerical explosion; regularization (e.g. Ridge / Tikhonov) stabilizes these systems.\n* **Tensor Core Acceleration**: Modern hardware accelerators (GPUs and TPUs) are specialized matrix multiplication engines; formulating operations linearly maximizes throughput.",
